{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a5e2ffce023de1c99","tool_use_id":"toolu_01XTw7T9hjfpEMDjcD8div9s","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus is **methods soundness** and **experimental rigor**. Review blindly — do not assume any outcome quality.\n\n## Venue Calibration (EMNLP 2026 ARR)\n- 5 = Award consideration (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference accept (~21% acceptance)\n- 3 = Findings accept\n- 2 = Major revision needed\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n\nDimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5)\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** OntoCoDec: Type-Valid Information Extraction via Compiled Ontology Constraints\n\n**Core idea:** LLM-based IE systems systematically violate ontology type constraints (13-57% domain/range violations). OntoCoDec compiles ontology schemas into two composable constraint layers for autoregressive decoding: (1) grammar layer via CFG/XGrammar enforcing structural/vocabulary constraints, (2) semantic layer with stateful entity registry + dynamic logit masking for cross-field domain/range constraints. Layers compose via bitwise AND with graceful degradation to grammar-only when intersection is empty.\n\n**Datasets:** SciERC (RE, 7 rel types, 551 test), DocRED (RE, 96 rel types, 998 test docs), WikiEvents (EE, 56 event types, 80 arg roles, 246 test docs)\n\n**Models:** Qwen3-8B, Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, Mistral-7B-v0.3, Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct (AWQ-INT4)\n\n## Key Results from Paper\n\n**Table 1 (ZS RE):**\n- SciERC DR reduction: Qwen 15.4→0.93%, Llama 13.3→0.08%, Mistral 56.9→13.4%\n- DocRED DR reduction: Qwen 18.5→10.2%, Llama 26.1→2.55%, Mistral 57.4→33.2%\n- R-F1 neutral (ΔR-F1: -0.14 to +0.13pp)\n- CF increases under constrained decoding (up to 9.90% SciERC, 12.02% DocRED)\n\n**Table 2 (Scale + FT):**\n- 70B ZS: DR→0.0% on both SciERC and DocRED, ΔR-F1 ≤+0.02pp\n- LoRA FT (8B): DR 0.00-0.21%, R-F1 40-46%\n- 70B LoRA (AWQ): highest R-F1 (46.2% SciERC, 45.0% DocRED) but slightly higher residual DR (0.20-0.50%)\n\n**Table 3 (ZS EE WikiEvents):**\n- Unconstrained: EV 10.5-37.3%, RoV 64-89%, Entity F1=0% for all models\n- Constrained: EV→0%, RoV→0%, Entity F1 recovers 11.7-17.6% (p<0.001), Trigger F1 +1.5-1.8pp\n- Arg F1 remains ~0% in ZS (KAIROS ontology complexity)\n- AC residual: 1.3-3.8%, CF elevated: 14.9-21.1%\n\n**Table 4 (Post-hoc filtering):**\n- RE: 16.8-48.1% rejection (functional to marginal)\n- EE: 99.6-100% rejection (non-viable)\n\n**Table 5 (EE FT composability):**\n- FT alone reduces RoV (Qwen 74.9→38.3%, Llama 88.8→8.8%) but doesn't eliminate\n- FT+OntoCoDec: RoV→0%, Arg F1 preserved (Qwen 7.9→8.2%, Llama 10.0→8.9%)\n- Trigger F1 decrease for Qwen (-5.4pp) but not Llama (-0.6pp)\n\n**Table 6 (Grammar-only ablation EE):**\n- Grammar eliminates EV→0% but RoV unchanged (64-77%)\n- AC rises from 0-0.3% to 8.2-14.3% (latent violation surfacing)\n\n**Table 7 (Latency):**\n- 10-13% overhead greedy, 16.5% beam-4\n- Ontology scalability: 7→200+ types, 2→3% overhead\n\n**Error analysis:**\n- AC residual: 72% semantic confusion, 28% BPE collision\n- CF: 85% entity-vs-relation type drift\n\n**Limitations section:** CF consistency, ZS F1 low, forward coreference out of scope\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (what was planned but may not be fully covered)\nThe full experiment plan included 14 experiments:\n- P021/P023/P025: WikiEvents ZS unconstrained baselines (3 models) — covered in Table 3\n- P027: WikiEvents Mistral grammar-only ablation — covered in Table 6\n- P028: Post-hoc filtering comparison — covered in Table 4\n- P029/P030: 70B ZS SciERC/DocRED — covered in Table 2\n- P031: Error analysis / case study — covered in text (Section 4.4)\n- P032: EE FT composability — covered in Table 5\n- P033: Grammar-only cross-model ablation (Qwen+Llama) — covered in Table 6\n- P034: Bootstrap CI for EE metrics — mentioned in text (p-values) but no full CI table\n- P035: Latency overhead — covered in Table 7\n- P029-LoRA/P030-LoRA: 70B LoRA SciERC/DocRED — covered in Table 2\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProduce a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Soundness Assessment** (score 1-5 with justification):\n   - Is the two-layer architecture well-motivated and correctly formalized?\n   - Is the graceful degradation mechanism principled?\n   - Are the experiments sufficient to support the claims?\n   - Statistical rigor (bootstrap CIs, p-values reported?)\n   - Are there methodological gaps?\n3. **Experimental Completeness** (detailed):\n   - Coverage of planned experiments\n   - Missing controls or baselines\n   - Are the violation metrics well-defined and reliable?\n   - Is the CF tradeoff adequately analyzed?\n   - Is the Mistral residual DR (13.4% SciERC, 33.2% DocRED) sufficiently explained?\n   - ZS F1 practical significance concern\n   - Post-hoc filtering comparison — is the argument sound?\n4. **Reproducibility** (score 1-5):\n   - Implementation details sufficient?\n   - Are prompts, hyperparameters, data splits described?\n5. **Excitement** (score 1-5)\n6. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific)\n7. **Questions for Authors** (3-5 specific questions)\n8. **Overall Assessment** (score on ARR 1-5 scale with 0.5 increments, with justification)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Calibrate to EMNLP 2026 standards (~21% main acceptance rate).","uuid":"bd09e5df-5250-425d-a963-8562d039f7e6","session_id":"a39f4bf6-f98d-43c7-bbf4-41a033e3e2e7"}
